# EXPERIMENTAL hardware-speed configuration (experimental build only).
#
# These variables are read ONLY by the experimental onvif_ptz_bridge build
# (compiled with -DPTZ_HW_SPEED). The stable build ignores them and contains no
# speed-ioctl code at all. Hardware speed is DISABLED by default; the bridge
# never issues the set-speed ioctl unless you explicitly enable it here.
#
# The verified speed ioctl is 0x40046d20 ("speed_step" in Hz, written directly;
# baseline 600). It programs the motor timer reload = 12_000_000/speed_step-1,
# applied at MOVE start (so the bridge sets it on axis_open, BEFORE the MOVE).
#
# IMPORTANT (verified by kernel RE, Nov 2026 - see ptz-kernel-timer-analysis/):
# raising speed_step may NOT raise real motion speed. At 600 the motor timer
# already delivers only ~500 IRQ/s, and ak_motor.ko advances current_pos only
# once per ~4 IRQs (an intentional divider), giving ~119 steps/s. A requested
# 750 was NOT shown to help (likely the ~500 IRQ/s service ceiling). Treat any
# value above 600 as an EXPERIMENT to MEASURE, not a known win. Do NOT exceed
# 750. Measure IRQ27/28 AND current_pos together (/tmp/ptz_measure_both.sh);
# keep a higher value only if BOTH rise and no steps are lost and Stop stays
# prompt. Revert on any abnormal behaviour (revert-to-stable.sh).
#
# To enable on the camera, export these before starting the experimental daemon
# (e.g. in enable-ptz.sh), never commit real values blindly:

#PTZ_HW_SPEED_ENABLED=0        # 1 to allow the bridge to set speed_step
#PTZ_PAN_SPEED_STEP=600        # pan speed_step when enabled (baseline 600)
#PTZ_TILT_SPEED_STEP=600       # tilt speed_step when enabled (baseline 600)

# The baseline (600) is restored on stop/close. Boundaries, margins, Stop
# priority and the watchdog are unchanged regardless of speed.
